append_md('SP61_CW_75_notes.md',r'''## Scan 019

The first paragraph completes the vector-envelope construction by twisting the standard polygon’s vertex inclusion by a dihedral torsor. The long NB compares functors out of the category of G-torsors with G-objects in a target category, then replaces the torsor category by the groupoid of objects isomorphic to a fixed E. The slanted margin states the connected-groupoid version. “DEA” labels the grouped note. No date or authorial page count; pencil archive number 18.

**Mathematics and reading.** The opening specifies “à n côtés,” not H’s “à isomorphisme près.” The first twist may have a prime on T; later twists visibly use T. The prime is not removed by demanding identical notation. The one-object expression is `(e,G)`, with a blunt connector; Hom is absent. In the NB the written target changes from C in Hom and the prose to script E in the cancelled/replacement action-category names. The superscript of Aut(X) is script E, not a circle. These category switches remain in the transcription. The definition near the middle has `G:Aut_C(E)`, two dots rather than an equals sign.

The lower diagram has separate solid and dashed diagonals. The solid rising line begins with a transverse bar; its evaluation label has another mapsto and a crossed subscript under the second Phi. The bottom margin uses E₀ in Aut(E₀), probably E₀ in the choice of object, but plain E in Phi(E). That last index is not supplied.

**Checks.** S019-TWIST checks that an equivariant injection remains injective after twisting by a nonempty torsor. S019-EVAL checks evaluation at the regular right torsor, including the left-action convention. The universal uniqueness phrase “de façon can. à isom unique près” is recorded as written; it has not been established as an unconditional uniqueness statement for all equivalences. The arrow label beneath the top shaft is still doubtful; it is not called an authorial question mark with certainty.''')
append_md('SP61_CW_75_doubts.md',r'''### Scan 019 — certainty-only decisions

The connector after `(e,G)` is blunt. The superscript on Aut(X) has the script-E outline. “foncteurs,” “équivalente,” “connexe,” and “d’équivalence” are readable; their C doubts are removed. The two dots in `G:Aut_C(E)` reverse C’s preference and therefore have a change row. The abbreviation ss/cat. and the crossed lower-diagram labels remain open.''')
append_md('SP61_CW_75_hand_log.md',r'''## Added on scan 019

- **C and script E are distinct locally, while C versus calligraphic C is not.** The action-category names and Aut(X) superscript have a middle inward turn and lower lobe; nearby C’s are open curves. H repeatedly replaces the former by C or a circle. This does not settle the stylistic alphabet of the C family.
- **Colons can replace expected equalities.** After G in the subcategory paragraph, the marks are two vertical dots. The equality expected from context is not transcribed.
- **Source bars and blunt tips recur.** The lower solid diagonal begins with a transverse bar; the slanted margin element correspondence has a bar and a rounded, headless end. C and H turn these into a two-headed arrow and mapsto respectively. Other arrows on the same sheet have unmistakable pointed heads, so the distinction remains local.
- **Small zero can collapse to a short dark dash.** The margin’s C₀ has rounder zeros than the doubtful E₀ just below it. No global rule treats every low dot as an index.
''')
checks([('S019-TWIST','targeted proof check','Choose a point t of the nonempty right D_n-torsor T. It identifies T×^{D_n}S(P_n) and T×^{D_n}E with S(P_n) and E, carrying the twisted map to the original injection. Thus the twisted vertex map is injective. This checks the construction, not the possible prime on the first displayed T.'),('S019-EVAL','targeted proof check','Automorphisms of the regular right G-torsor G_d are left multiplications L_g, with L_g L_h=L_{gh}. Evaluating a covariant functor at G_d therefore gives a left G-action. For each right torsor P, the isomorphisms G_d→P identify its functor value with the twist of that G-object. Naturality then identifies transformations with G-equivariant maps. This verifies the stated equivalence at the usual category/groupoid level.'),('S019-UNIQUE','carried as background without fault found','The words “de façon can. à isom unique près” are transcribed but no universal uniqueness theorem for arbitrary chosen equivalences was proved in this run.')])
